          body:
            "You were asked to add pagination to the students list, and while in there you fixed a typo, renamed a variable, upgraded a dependency and changed the colour of the pagination buttons — all in your working directory. Before the PR, reshape it.\n\n**Step 1 — inventory the diff.** `git diff` and list the *logical* changes:\n\n```\nA. pagination: new query params, sliced response, prev/next links\nB. refactor: rename `data` → `students` in the list route (no behaviour change)\nC. fix: typo in the error message of the old endpoint\nD. chore: bump `qs` dependency (security fix)\nE. style: pagination button colour (CSS only)\n```\n\n**Step 2 — order by dependency and risk.** Chores and pure refactors first (they change no behaviour, so anything that breaks after is *their* fault, isolated), then the fix, then the feature, then the style:\n\n```\ncommit 1 (chore):   Bump qs to 6.11 — fixes prototype pollution advisory\ncommit 2 (refactor):Rename `data` to `students` in list routes\ncommit 3 (fix):      Correct 500 message on malformed query\ncommit 4 (feat):     Add cursor pagination to student list\ncommit 5 (style):    Distinguish pagination controls from links\n```\n\n**Step 3 — stage in slices.** The working directory holds all five changes; `git add -p` (patch mode) lets you stage *hunks* — or stage whole files per commit if the changes live in different files. For edits intertwined in one file, stage interactively; for a refactor so large it cannot be split sensibly, `git stash` the feature work, commit the refactor alone, then unstash.\n\n**Step 4 — verify each step.** After each commit: does it build? Commit 2 (rename) must be behaviour-neutral — run the tests; if they fail, the rename caught a hidden dependency and you have found a bug *for free*, at the exact commit that caused it.\n\n**Step 5 — the PR reads as a story.** Reviewers see five commits whose messages explain themselves; the diff of commit 4 contains *only* the pagination behaviour, so its review is about pagination; bisect now pinpoints regressions to the exact change; and if the pagination must be rolled back, reverting *that one commit* leaves the dependency fix and rename intact. **Compare the alternative**: one commit `\"stuff + pagination\"` where every one of those five values is destroyed — the reviewer reads 900 mixed lines, the rollback is all-or-nothing, and `git blame` on any line points at a message that says nothing.\n\n**The test for \"is my history good enough?\"**: could you `git revert` any single commit without collateral damage, and could a stranger reading the log infer *why* each change happened? If yes, the history is a tool; if no, it is a diary written in code.",
